    overview:
      "Welcome to Spanish contract law. Spanish contract law runs on the 1889 Código Civil, still largely intact after well over a century, and unlike France, which dropped its equivalent doctrine in 2016, Spain still requires a valid causa for a contract to be enforceable at all. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a real Spanish contract dispute actually unfolds. We start at the threshold, the three requirements Article 1261 demands for a valid contract, who has the capacity to meet them, and who can act on another's behalf in doing so. We then cover the formalities the law demands for specific transactions, and the ways a validly formed contract can still unravel through a defect in one party's actual consent. We turn next to what happens once performance goes wrong: the remedy menu Spanish law makes available, and the narrow, judge-made doctrine that lets a court revise a contract for genuinely extraordinary hardship. We turn to Spain's own globally significant contribution to unfair-terms law, the mortgage floor-clause litigation that shaped how transparency itself gets tested across Europe, and to the clock that eventually runs out on every claim. We close with the principle that has quietly shaped everything covered before it: the general, codified duty of good faith running through the performance of every contract. Keep one comparison running underneath everything you hear: where Spain's civil-law neighbor France modernized a given doctrine, ask whether Spain kept the older rule, and what that divergence actually costs a lawyer who assumes the two systems still work identically.",
