        id: "es-contract-consentimiento-objeto-causa",
        title: "Consentimiento, objeto, causa — the three requirements of Article 1261",
        explanation:
          "Start at the threshold every contract has to cross before anything else in this lecture becomes relevant. Article 1261 of the Código Civil requires three elements for a valid contract: consentimiento, consent, meaning a matching offer and acceptance freely given by parties capable of understanding what they are agreeing to; objeto, a lawful, possible, and determinate object, the actual thing or service the contract concerns, which cannot be something outside commerce, impossible to perform, or so vague that a court could never actually enforce it; and causa, a valid underlying cause, the legal justification the law demands for every obligation, whether causa onerosa in an ordinary exchange contract, causa remuneratoria for services already rendered, or causa de pura beneficencia for a genuine gift. Article 1275 goes further and declares that a contract with no causa at all, or with an unlawful one, produces no legal effect whatsoever. Unlike France, whose 2016 reform of the Code civil deliberately abandoned cause as a standalone doctrinal category in favor of folding its functions into content and proportionality review, Spain retained causa as a distinct, independent formal requirement, which means the two neighboring civil-law systems no longer analyze contract validity through an identical doctrinal lens, however similar their nineteenth-century origins once were.",
        whyItMatters:
          "This is a live doctrinal difference between two civil-law neighbors, and you should never assume Spanish and French contract law work identically just because both descend from similar Napoleonic-era codes: Spain's 1889 code kept causa exactly where France's 2016 reform explicitly dropped it, so a French-trained lawyer importing French analysis into a Spanish file risks missing an entire independent ground of invalidity [1]. You should treat causa as a genuine, independent ground of invalidity distinct from an unlawful objeto, since a contract can have a perfectly lawful object while still failing for lacking any genuine underlying causa at all, a sham transaction dressed up to look like an ordinary exchange. You should also remember that Spanish courts distinguish a simulación absoluta, where no real contract was ever actually intended, from a simulación relativa, where the parties disguise one genuine contract as another, since the two produce meaningfully different consequences for what, if anything, a court will actually enforce once the disguise is uncovered.",
