        explanation:
          "Now, turn to who is actually capable of giving the consentimiento Article 1261 requires. Spanish law sets eighteen as the general age of full contractual capacity under Article 322 of the Código Civil, with specific emancipación rules allowing minors from sixteen to gain expanded, though not entirely full, legal capacity under defined circumstances: through parental concession recorded before the Registro Civil, through judicial concession where a minor lives independently from parental authority, or automatically through marriage. Separate rules govern contracts by persons whose capacity to make certain decisions has been judicially modified, previously and still sometimes colloquially called incapacitados, a framework and terminology comprehensively reformed by Ley 8/2021 to emphasize apoyos, support measures tailored to the individual, rather than a single blanket declaration of incapacity followed by a substitute decision-maker acting entirely in the person's place. A contract concluded by someone lacking the requisite capacity is not automatically void outright but is anulable, voidable, meaning it produces effects until the party with the protected interest, or their legal representative, actually seeks its annulment within the statutory period. Foreign nationals contracting in Spain, meanwhile, generally have their capacity governed by their own national law under Article 9 of the Código Civil's private international law rules, a further layer worth checking whenever a party to a Spanish contract is not Spanish.",

