          "Now, turn to how a contract that formed validly, met whatever form it required, and was concluded by parties with genuine capacity and authority, can still unravel. Articles 1265 through 1270 of the Código Civil recognize four vicios del consentimiento, defects of consent, that render a contract voidable, anulable, rather than automatically void: error, a genuine mistake about the substance of the thing contracted for or about a condition that was the principal motive for entering the contract; dolo, fraud, a deliberate deception, including insidious words or machinations used to induce the other party's consent, distinguished from the milder dolo incidental that affects only the terms rather than the decision to contract at all; violencia, physical, irresistible force used to extract consent; and intimidación, the threat of imminent and serious harm to a person, their spouse, or close relatives, or their property, used to the same end. A contract affected by any of these defects can be annulled through an acción de nulidad relativa at the request of the party whose consent was actually defective, generally within four years under Article 1301, running from the moment the defect ceased for violencia and intimidación, or from the contract's own conclusion for error and dolo.",
