    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where Spain's civil-law neighbor France modernized a given doctrine, ask whether Spain kept the older rule, and what that divergence actually costs a lawyer who assumes the two systems still work identically. Consentimiento, objeto, and causa, together with capacidad and la representación, answer the threshold question of whether a valid contract exists at all, and who was actually authorized to form it, Spain's retention of causa under Article 1261 a genuine point of divergence from France's more modernized 2016 approach to the same underlying doctrinal territory [1]. La forma contractual layers formality requirements onto specific transaction types, generally as an evidentiary rather than validity requirement, while vicios del consentimiento is how an otherwise validly formed contract can still unravel for a genuine defect in one party's actual consent, error, dolo, violencia, or intimidación. The Article 1124 remedy menu governs what happens once performance goes wrong, giving the aggrieved party a genuine choice between specific performance and termination, and rebus sic stantibus addresses the narrower case of a contract that becomes ruinous through nobody's fault at all, a judge-made doctrine considerably less codified than its French counterpart. Cláusulas abusivas, illustrated vividly by the cláusulas suelo saga, is the separate control on unfair standard terms, turning specifically on transparency rather than on substance alone, and la prescripción is the clock running underneath every one of these remedies the whole time, dramatically shortened by the 2015 reform. Buena fe, finally, is the pervasive principle running underneath everything else in this lecture, from causa at the very start through to the transparency standard that decided Spain's own most consequential recent consumer case. As you read any Spanish contract dispute from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and never assume a rule you learned for France or Germany transfers automatically to Spain.",
