    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where Germany's federalism is uniform, ask what Spain's own asymmetric version actually looks like instead, and where a rule was designed as a negotiated compromise rather than a clean theoretical structure, read it with that historical origin in mind. The 1978 Constitution's negotiated-transition origins shaped the monarquía parlamentaria it preserved as a unifying compromise, and las Cortes Generales, with the investidura process covered alongside it, is the elected institution actually holding the governing power the monarchy formally, but only formally, channels. El Estado de las Autonomías and each community's specific Estatuto de Autonomía implement Spain's asymmetric territorial devolution, and el bloque de constitucionalidad is the layered set of texts, Estatutos included, the Tribunal Constitucional actually applies when resolving the competency disputes that devolution inevitably produces. The Tribunal Constitucional is the institution enforcing the whole framework's limits, via recurso de amparo for individual rights and its other jurisdictions for structural questions, and estado de alarma, excepción, and sitio show what happens to this framework under genuine crisis conditions. The ongoing Catalan territorial tension is the framework's own real stress test, still actively unresolved, and the primacía del derecho de la Unión Europea, finally, adds a further, still-evolving layer on top of the entire domestic structure this lecture has covered. As you read any Spanish constitutional or regulatory question from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and resist the reflex to assume a rule you learned for Germany's uniform federalism transfers automatically to Spain's genuinely asymmetric one.",
